(window.DVR_PAPERS=window.DVR_PAPERS||{})["cs-classes-2027q-103"]={"v":1,"id":"cs-classes-2027q-103","title":"DVRUO Classes GATE CS | Theory of Computation | Countability | Weekly Quiz 11","sourceTitle":"DVRUO Classes GATE CS | Theory of Computation | Countability | Weekly Quiz 11","branch":"COMPUTER SCIENCE ENGINEERING","code":"CS","provider":"DVRUO CLASSES","providerKey":"classes","series":"DVRUO Classes Weekly Quizzes for GATE CSE 2027","seriesId":"cs-classes-2027q","num":103,"category":"other","year":"","format":"B","durationSec":1800,"info":{"orgName":"DVRUO Classes","batchName":"DVRUO Classes Weekly Quizzes for GATE CSE 2027","subjectName":"Theory of Computation"},"source":"COMPUTER SCIENCE ENGINEERING/GO CLASSES/GO Classes Weekly Quizzes for GATE CSE 2027/011_exam_2414_GO Classes GATE CS Theory of Computation Countability Weekly Quiz 11.html","questions":[{"n":1,"type":"MSQ","section":"Technical","tags":"","html":"<p>Let $A$ be a non-empty countable set, and let $f: B \\rightarrow A$ be a function from an arbitrary set $B$ to $A$. For each element $x \\in A$, let the preimage of $x$ under $f$ be denoted as:<br>$$<br>f^{-1}(x)=\\{b \\in B \\mid f(b)=x\\}<br>$$<br>If $f^{-1}(x)$ is a countable set for every $x \\in A$, which of the following is the correct conclusion regarding the<br>cardinality of the domain set $B$?</p>","paragraph":null,"options":[{"key":"A","html":"<p>$B$ must be an uncountably infinite set.<br>&nbsp;</p>"},{"key":"B","html":"<p>$B$ must be a finite set.<br>&nbsp;</p>"},{"key":"C","html":"<p>$B$ is guaranteed to be a countable set.<br>&nbsp;</p>"},{"key":"D","html":"<p>The cardinality of $B$ cannot be determined.</p>"}],"answer":["A"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"A","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0},"solution":"<p><strong>Answer:</strong> A</p><div class=\"source-solutions\" data-answer-source=\"inferred\"><section><h4>Source solution 1 <small>(score: 0)</small></h4><a name=\"532610\"></a><div itemprop=\"text\"><p>Since<br>$$<br>B=\\bigcup_{x \\in A} f^{-1}(x),<br>$$<br>the set $B$ is a union of the preimages $f^{-1}(x)$.<br>Now, for every $x \\in A$, the set $f^{-1}(x)$ is countable by assumption. Also, $A$ itself is countable.<br><br>Hence, $B$ is a countable union of countable sets and countable union of countable sets is always countable. Therefore, $B$ is countable.<br><br>This is alternate definition of countable set. Learn this new definition:</p><div style=\"text-align: center;\"><p><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer nofollow\" style=\"display: inline-block; background-color: rgb(0, 140, 186); color: white; padding: 10px 20px; text-align: center; text-decoration: none; font-size: 16px; border-radius: 6px; border-width: medium; border-style: none; border-color: currentcolor; border-image: initial; font-family: sans-serif; --darkreader-inline-bgcolor: var(--darkreader-background-008cba, #007095); --darkreader-inline-color: var(--darkreader-text-ffffff, #e8e6e3);\" data-darkreader-inline-bgcolor=\"\" data-darkreader-inline-color=\"\" href=\"https://youtu.be/9y_W6Rgxovs?list=PLIPZ2_p3RNHgXosiQv-gL1PvJkcHokW1p&amp;t=3915\">Watch here! </a> &nbsp;&nbsp;</p><div style=\"text-align: center;\"><p><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer nofollow\" style=\"display: inline-block; background-color: rgb(0, 140, 186); color: white; padding: 10px 20px; text-align: center; text-decoration: none; font-size: 16px; border-radius: 6px; border-width: medium; border-style: none; border-color: currentcolor; border-image: initial; font-family: sans-serif; --darkreader-inline-bgcolor: var(--darkreader-background-008cba, #007095); --darkreader-inline-color: var(--darkreader-text-ffffff, #e8e6e3);\" data-darkreader-inline-bgcolor=\"\" data-darkreader-inline-color=\"\" href=\"https://youtu.be/w8L1pBaD1oo?t=3052\">Countable union of countable sets is always countable</a> &nbsp;&nbsp;</p></div></div></div></section><section><h4>Source solution 2 <small>(score: 0)</small></h4><a name=\"535533\"></a><div itemprop=\"text\"><div class=\"markdown markdown-main-panel stronger enable-updated-hr-color\" style=\"--animation-duration: 400ms; --fade-animation-function: ease-out; font-family: Google Sans Text, sans-serif !important; line-height: 1.15 !important; margin-top: 0px !important;\" id=\"model-response-message-contentr_dc7cd8b177cb1ea4\" aria-live=\"polite\" aria-busy=\"false\" dir=\"ltr\"><h3 data-path-to-node=\"4\" style=\"font-family: Google Sans, sans-serif !important; line-height: 1.15 !important; margin-top: 0px !important;\"><b data-path-to-node=\"4\" data-index-in-node=\"0\" style=\"font-family: Google Sans Text, sans-serif !important; line-height: 1.15 !important; margin-top: 0px !important;\">Breaking Down the Logic</b></h3><p data-path-to-node=\"5\" style=\"font-family: Google Sans Text, sans-serif !important; line-height: 1.15 !important; margin-top: 0px !important;\">To understand why <span class=\"math-inline\" data-math=\"B\" data-index-in-node=\"18\" style=\"font-family: Google Sans Text, sans-serif !important; line-height: 1.15 !important; margin-top: 0px !important;\">$B$</span> is guaranteed to be a countable set, we need to look at how a function divides its domain.</p><h4 data-path-to-node=\"6\" style=\"font-family: Google Sans, sans-serif !important; line-height: 1.15 !important; margin-top: 0px !important;\"><b data-path-to-node=\"6\" data-index-in-node=\"0\" style=\"font-family: Google Sans Text, sans-serif !important; line-height: 1.15 !important; margin-top: 0px !important;\">1. The Domain as a Union of Preimages</b></h4><p data-path-to-node=\"7\" style=\"font-family: Google Sans Text, sans-serif !important; line-height: 1.15 !important; margin-top: 0px !important;\">By definition, every single element <span class=\"math-inline\" data-math=\"b \\in B\" data-index-in-node=\"36\" style=\"font-family: Google Sans Text, sans-serif !important; line-height: 1.15 !important; margin-top: 0px !important;\">$b \\in B$</span> maps to some element <span class=\"math-inline\" data-math=\"x \\in A\" data-index-in-node=\"65\" style=\"font-family: Google Sans Text, sans-serif !important; line-height: 1.15 !important; margin-top: 0px !important;\">$x \\in A$</span>. Therefore, if we collect the preimages <span class=\"math-inline\" data-math=\"f^{-1}(x)\" data-index-in-node=\"113\" style=\"font-family: Google Sans Text, sans-serif !important; line-height: 1.15 !important; margin-top: 0px !important;\">$f^{-1}(x)$</span> for every single <span class=\"math-inline\" data-math=\"x\" data-index-in-node=\"140\" style=\"font-family: Google Sans Text, sans-serif !important; line-height: 1.15 !important; margin-top: 0px !important;\">$x$</span> in the codomain <span class=\"math-inline\" data-math=\"A\" data-index-in-node=\"158\" style=\"font-family: Google Sans Text, sans-serif !important; line-height: 1.15 !important; margin-top: 0px !important;\">$A$</span>, their union will give us back the entire domain <span class=\"math-inline\" data-math=\"B\" data-index-in-node=\"209\" style=\"font-family: Google Sans Text, sans-serif !important; line-height: 1.15 !important; margin-top: 0px !important;\">$B$</span>:</p><div data-path-to-node=\"8\" style=\"font-family: Google Sans Text, sans-serif !important; line-height: 1.15 !important; margin-top: 0px !important;\"><div class=\"math-block\" data-math=\"B = \\bigcup_{x \\in A} f^{-1}(x)\" style=\"font-family: Google Sans Text, sans-serif !important; line-height: 1.15 !important; margin-top: 0px !important;\">$$B = \\bigcup_{x \\in A} f^{-1}(x)$$</div></div><h4 data-path-to-node=\"9\" style=\"font-family: Google Sans, sans-serif !important; line-height: 1.15 !important; margin-top: 0px !important;\"><b data-path-to-node=\"9\" data-index-in-node=\"0\" style=\"font-family: Google Sans Text, sans-serif !important; line-height: 1.15 !important; margin-top: 0px !important;\">2. Analyzing the Sizes</b></h4><p data-path-to-node=\"10\" style=\"font-family: Google Sans Text, sans-serif !important; line-height: 1.15 !important; margin-top: 0px !important;\">Now, let's look at the two pieces of information given in the problem:</p><ol start=\"1\" data-path-to-node=\"11\" style=\"padding-inline-start: 32px; font-family: Google Sans Text, sans-serif !important; line-height: 1.15 !important; margin-top: 0px !important;\"><li style=\"font-family: Google Sans Text, sans-serif !important; line-height: 1.15 !important; margin-top: 0px !important;\"><p data-path-to-node=\"11,0,0\" style=\"font-family: Google Sans Text, sans-serif !important; line-height: 1.15 !important; margin-top: 0px !important;\"><b data-path-to-node=\"11,0,0\" data-index-in-node=\"0\" style=\"font-family: Google Sans Text, sans-serif !important; line-height: 1.15 !important; margin-top: 0px !important;\">The index set <span class=\"math-inline\" data-math=\"A\" data-index-in-node=\"14\" style=\"font-family: Google Sans Text, sans-serif !important; line-height: 1.15 !important; margin-top: 0px !important;\">$A$</span> is countable.</b> This means we are taking a union over a countable number of sets.</p></li><li style=\"font-family: Google Sans Text, sans-serif !important; line-height: 1.15 !important; margin-top: 0px !important;\"><p data-path-to-node=\"11,1,0\" style=\"font-family: Google Sans Text, sans-serif !important; line-height: 1.15 !important; margin-top: 0px !important;\"><b data-path-to-node=\"11,1,0\" data-index-in-node=\"0\" style=\"font-family: Google Sans Text, sans-serif !important; line-height: 1.15 !important; margin-top: 0px !important;\">Each preimage <span class=\"math-inline\" data-math=\"f^{-1}(x)\" data-index-in-node=\"14\" style=\"font-family: Google Sans Text, sans-serif !important; line-height: 1.15 !important; margin-top: 0px !important;\">$f^{-1}(x)$</span> is a countable set</b> for every <span class=\"math-inline\" data-math=\"x \\in A\" data-index-in-node=\"53\" style=\"font-family: Google Sans Text, sans-serif !important; line-height: 1.15 !important; margin-top: 0px !important;\">$x \\in A$</span>.</p></li></ol><h4 data-path-to-node=\"12\" style=\"font-family: Google Sans, sans-serif !important; line-height: 1.15 !important; margin-top: 0px !important;\"><b data-path-to-node=\"12\" data-index-in-node=\"0\" style=\"font-family: Google Sans Text, sans-serif !important; line-height: 1.15 !important; margin-top: 0px !important;\">3. The Countable Union Theorem</b></h4><p data-path-to-node=\"13\" style=\"font-family: Google Sans Text, sans-serif !important; line-height: 1.15 !important; margin-top: 0px !important;\">There is a fundamental theorem in set theory that perfectly answers this:</p><blockquote data-path-to-node=\"14\" style=\"font-family: Google Sans Text, sans-serif !important; line-height: 1.15 !important; margin-top: 0px !important;\"><p data-path-to-node=\"14,0\" style=\"font-family: Google Sans Text, sans-serif !important; line-height: 1.15 !important; margin-top: 0px !important;\"><b data-path-to-node=\"14,0\" data-index-in-node=\"0\" style=\"font-family: Google Sans Text, sans-serif !important; line-height: 1.15 !important; margin-top: 0px !important;\">Theorem:</b> The countable union of countable sets is always countable.</p></blockquote><p data-path-to-node=\"15\" style=\"font-family: Google Sans Text, sans-serif !important; line-height: 1.15 !important; margin-top: 0px !important;\">Since we are combining a countable number of countable preimages, the resulting set <span class=\"math-inline\" data-math=\"B\" data-index-in-node=\"84\" style=\"font-family: Google Sans Text, sans-serif !important; line-height: 1.15 !important; margin-top: 0px !important;\">$B$</span> cannot suddenly become uncountably infinite.</p><h3 data-path-to-node=\"17\" style=\"font-family: Google Sans, sans-serif !important; line-height: 1.15 !important; margin-top: 0px !important;\"><b data-path-to-node=\"17\" data-index-in-node=\"0\" style=\"font-family: Google Sans Text, sans-serif !important; line-height: 1.15 !important; margin-top: 0px !important;\">Why the Other Options are Incorrect</b></h3><ul data-path-to-node=\"18\" style=\"padding-inline-start: 32px; font-family: Google Sans Text, sans-serif !important; line-height: 1.15 !important; margin-top: 0px !important;\"><li style=\"font-family: Google Sans Text, sans-serif !important; line-height: 1.15 !important; margin-top: 0px !important;\"><p data-path-to-node=\"18,0,0\" style=\"font-family: Google Sans Text, sans-serif !important; line-height: 1.15 !important; margin-top: 0px !important;\"><b data-path-to-node=\"18,0,0\" data-index-in-node=\"0\" style=\"font-family: Google Sans Text, sans-serif !important; line-height: 1.15 !important; margin-top: 0px !important;\">A is incorrect:</b> As proven above, it's mathematically impossible for <span class=\"math-inline\" data-math=\"B\" data-index-in-node=\"68\" style=\"font-family: Google Sans Text, sans-serif !important; line-height: 1.15 !important; margin-top: 0px !important;\">$B$</span> to be uncountably infinite under these conditions.</p></li><li style=\"font-family: Google Sans Text, sans-serif !important; line-height: 1.15 !important; margin-top: 0px !important;\"><p data-path-to-node=\"18,1,0\" style=\"font-family: Google Sans Text, sans-serif !important; line-height: 1.15 !important; margin-top: 0px !important;\"><b data-path-to-node=\"18,1,0\" data-index-in-node=\"0\" style=\"font-family: Google Sans Text, sans-serif !important; line-height: 1.15 !important; margin-top: 0px !important;\">B is incorrect (<span class=\"math-inline\" data-math=\"B\" data-index-in-node=\"16\" style=\"font-family: Google Sans Text, sans-serif !important; line-height: 1.15 !important; margin-top: 0px !important;\">$B$</span> must be a finite set):</b> <span class=\"math-inline\" data-math=\"B\" data-index-in-node=\"41\" style=\"font-family: Google Sans Text, sans-serif !important; line-height: 1.15 !important; margin-top: 0px !important;\">$B$</span> <i data-path-to-node=\"18,1,0\" data-index-in-node=\"43\" style=\"font-family: Google Sans Text, sans-serif !important; line-height: 1.15 !important; margin-top: 0px !important;\">could</i> be finite, but it isn't <i data-path-to-node=\"18,1,0\" data-index-in-node=\"73\" style=\"font-family: Google Sans Text, sans-serif !important; line-height: 1.15 !important; margin-top: 0px !important;\">forced</i> to be. For example, if <span class=\"math-inline\" data-math=\"A = \\mathbb{N}\" data-index-in-node=\"103\" style=\"font-family: Google Sans Text, sans-serif !important; line-height: 1.15 !important; margin-top: 0px !important;\">$A = \\mathbb{N}$</span> (countable) and each <span class=\"math-inline\" data-math=\"f^{-1}(x) = \\{x\\}\" data-index-in-node=\"139\" style=\"font-family: Google Sans Text, sans-serif !important; line-height: 1.15 !important; margin-top: 0px !important;\">$f^{-1}(x) = \\{x\\}$</span> (a finite, and therefore countable set), then <span class=\"math-inline\" data-math=\"B = \\mathbb{N}\" data-index-in-node=\"203\" style=\"font-family: Google Sans Text, sans-serif !important; line-height: 1.15 !important; margin-top: 0px !important;\">$B = \\mathbb{N}$</span>, which is countably infinite, not finite.</p></li><li style=\"font-family: Google Sans Text, sans-serif !important; line-height: 1.15 !important; margin-top: 0px !important;\"><p data-path-to-node=\"18,2,0\" style=\"font-family: Google Sans Text, sans-serif !important; line-height: 1.15 !important; margin-top: 0px !important;\"><b data-path-to-node=\"18,2,0\" data-index-in-node=\"0\" style=\"font-family: Google Sans Text, sans-serif !important; line-height: 1.15 !important; margin-top: 0px !important;\">D is incorrect:</b> We can definitively determine that <span class=\"math-inline\" data-math=\"B\" data-index-in-node=\"51\" style=\"font-family: Google Sans Text, sans-serif !important; line-height: 1.15 !important; margin-top: 0px !important;\">$B$</span> is bounded by countability.</p></li></ul><h3 data-path-to-node=\"19\" style=\"font-family: Google Sans, sans-serif !important; line-height: 1.15 !important; margin-top: 0px !important;\"><b data-path-to-node=\"19\" data-index-in-node=\"0\" style=\"font-family: Google Sans Text, sans-serif !important; line-height: 1.15 !important; margin-top: 0px !important;\">Conclusion</b></h3><p data-path-to-node=\"20\" style=\"font-family: Google Sans Text, sans-serif !important; line-height: 1.15 !important; margin-top: 0px !important;\">Because <span class=\"math-inline\" data-math=\"B\" data-index-in-node=\"8\" style=\"font-family: Google Sans Text, sans-serif !important; line-height: 1.15 !important; margin-top: 0px !important;\">$B$</span> is a countable union of countable sets, <b data-path-to-node=\"20\" data-index-in-node=\"50\" style=\"font-family: Google Sans Text, sans-serif !important; line-height: 1.15 !important; margin-top: 0px !important;\">Option C</b> is the only guaranteed conclusion.</p></div></div></section></div><p><a href=\"https://gateoverflow.in/532609\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Official discussion page</a></p>"},{"n":2,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"","html":"<p>Consider the following two mathematical assertions regarding an arbitrary set $Y$, where $|Y|$ denotes the cardinality of $Y, Y \\times Y$ denotes its Cartesian product, and $\\mathcal{P}(Y)$ denotes its power set:<br><br>$\\textbf{Statement I:}$ There exists a set $Y$ such that $|Y| \\neq|Y \\times Y|$.<br><br>$\\textbf{Statement II:}$ There exists a set $Y$ such that $|Y| \\neq|\\mathcal{P}(Y)|$.<br><br>Which of the following option is correct?</p>","paragraph":null,"options":[{"key":"A","html":"<p>Both Statement $\\mathbf{I}$ and Statement $\\mathbf{II}$ are false.<br>&nbsp;</p>"},{"key":"B","html":"<p>Statement $\\mathbf{I}$&nbsp;is true, but Statement $\\mathbf{II}$ is false.<br>&nbsp;</p>"},{"key":"C","html":"<p>Statement $\\mathbf{I}$&nbsp;is false, but Statement $\\mathbf{II}$&nbsp;is true.<br>&nbsp;</p>"},{"key":"D","html":"<p>Both Statement $\\mathbf{I}$&nbsp;and Statement $\\mathbf{II}$ are true.</p>"}],"answer":[],"natRange":null,"answerText":"","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0.3333},"solution":"<p><strong>Answer:</strong> Not available</p><div class=\"source-solutions\" data-answer-source=\"solution_only\"><section><h4>Source solution 1 <small>(score: 0)</small></h4><a name=\"535549\"></a><div itemprop=\"text\">Statement I. There exists a set $Y$ such that $|Y| \\neq|Y \\times Y|$.<br>\n<br>\nTrue. Any finite set with cardinality greater than 1 will work here. For instance, take $Y = \\{1, 2, 3 \\}$.<br>\n<br>\n$|Y | = 3$ and $|Y \\times Y| = 9 \\Longrightarrow |Y | \\neq |Y \\times Y|$<br>\n<br>\nStatement II. There exists a set $Y$ such that $|Y| \\neq|\\mathcal{P}(Y)|$.<br>\n<br>\nTrue. In fact this is true for every set.</div></section></div><p><a href=\"https://gateoverflow.in/532608\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Official discussion page</a></p>","keyMissing":true},{"n":3,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"","html":"<p>Let $A$ and $B$ be two non-empty sets. Consider the following three assertions expressed in first-order logic symbols, where $|A|$ and $|B|$ denote the cardinalities of sets $A$ and $B$, respectively:</p><p>$\\mathbf{I.}$ $\\exists f: A \\rightarrow B \\quad \\forall a_{1} \\in A \\quad \\forall a_{2} \\in A\\left(a_{1} \\neq a_{2} \\rightarrow f\\left(a_{1}\\right) \\neq f\\left(a_{2}\\right)\\right)$</p><p>$\\mathbf{II.}$ $\\exists f: A \\rightarrow B \\quad \\forall b \\in B \\quad \\exists a \\in A(f(a)=b)$</p><p>$\\mathbf{III.}$ $\\exists f: A \\rightarrow B \\quad \\forall b \\in B \\quad \\exists a \\in A\\left(f(a)=b \\wedge \\forall a^{\\prime} \\in A\\left(a \\neq a^{\\prime} \\rightarrow f\\left(a^{\\prime}\\right) \\neq b\\right)\\right)$</p><p><br>Which of the following describes the set cardinality relationships implied by Statements $\\mathbf{I}$, $\\mathbf{II}$, and $\\mathbf{III}$ in order?</p><p>&nbsp;</p>","paragraph":null,"options":[{"key":"A","html":"<p>$\\mathbf{I:}$&nbsp;$|A| \\geq|B|, \\quad$ $\\mathbf{II:}$ $|A| \\leq|B|, \\quad$ $\\mathbf{III:}$ $|A|=|B|$<br>&nbsp;</p>"},{"key":"B","html":"<p>$\\mathbf{I:}$ $|A| \\leq|B|, \\quad$ $\\mathbf{II:}$ $|A| \\geq|B|, \\quad$ $\\mathbf{III:}$ $|A|=|B|$<br>&nbsp;</p>"},{"key":"C","html":"<p>$\\mathbf{I:}$&nbsp; $|A| \\leq|B|, \\quad$ $\\mathbf{II:}$ $|A| \\leq|B|, \\quad$ $\\mathbf{III:}$ $|A| \\neq|B|$<br>&nbsp;</p>"},{"key":"D","html":"<p>$\\mathbf{I:}$ $|A|=|B|, \\quad$ $\\mathbf{II:}$ $|A| \\geq|B|, \\quad$ $\\mathbf{III:}$ $|A| \\leq|B|$</p>"}],"answer":[],"natRange":null,"answerText":"","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0.3333},"solution":"<p><strong>Answer:</strong> Not available</p><div class=\"source-solutions\" data-answer-source=\"solution_only\"><section><h4>Source solution 1 <small>(score: 0)</small></h4><a name=\"535530\"></a><div itemprop=\"text\"><div class=\"markdown markdown-main-panel stronger enable-updated-hr-color\" style=\"--animation-duration: 400ms; --fade-animation-function: ease-out; font-family: Google Sans Text, sans-serif !important; line-height: 1.15 !important; margin-top: 0px !important;\" id=\"model-response-message-contentr_de65ff7dd4eb7d7b\" aria-live=\"polite\" aria-busy=\"false\" dir=\"ltr\"><h3 data-path-to-node=\"2\" style=\"font-family: Google Sans, sans-serif !important; line-height: 1.15 !important; margin-top: 0px !important;\"><b data-path-to-node=\"2\" data-index-in-node=\"0\" style=\"font-family: Google Sans Text, sans-serif !important; line-height: 1.15 !important; margin-top: 0px !important;\">Understanding the Logic Statements</b></h3><p data-path-to-node=\"3\" style=\"font-family: Google Sans Text, sans-serif !important; line-height: 1.15 !important; margin-top: 0px !important;\">The question asks us to identify what the three first-order logic assertions mean regarding the sizes (cardinalities) of sets <span class=\"math-inline\" data-math=\"A\" data-index-in-node=\"126\" style=\"font-family: Google Sans Text, sans-serif !important; line-height: 1.15 !important; margin-top: 0px !important;\">$A$</span> and <span class=\"math-inline\" data-math=\"B\" data-index-in-node=\"132\" style=\"font-family: Google Sans Text, sans-serif !important; line-height: 1.15 !important; margin-top: 0px !important;\">$B$</span>. Let's break down each statement step-by-step.</p><h4 data-path-to-node=\"5\" style=\"font-family: Google Sans, sans-serif !important; line-height: 1.15 !important; margin-top: 0px !important;\"><b data-path-to-node=\"5\" data-index-in-node=\"0\" style=\"font-family: Google Sans Text, sans-serif !important; line-height: 1.15 !important; margin-top: 0px !important;\">Statement I: Injection (One-to-One)</b></h4><div data-path-to-node=\"6\" style=\"font-family: Google Sans Text, sans-serif !important; line-height: 1.15 !important; margin-top: 0px !important;\"><div class=\"math-block\" data-math=\"\\mathbf{I}. \\quad \\exists f : A \\to B \\quad \\forall a_1 \\in A \\quad \\forall a_2 \\in A \\, (a_1 \\neq a_2 \\to f(a_1) \\neq f(a_2))\" style=\"font-family: Google Sans Text, sans-serif !important; line-height: 1.15 !important; margin-top: 0px !important;\">$$\\mathbf{I}. \\quad \\exists f : A \\to B \\quad \\forall a_1 \\in A \\quad \\forall a_2 \\in A \\, (a_1 \\neq a_2 \\to f(a_1) \\neq f(a_2))$$</div></div><ul data-path-to-node=\"7\" style=\"padding-inline-start: 32px; font-family: Google Sans Text, sans-serif !important; line-height: 1.15 !important; margin-top: 0px !important;\"><li style=\"font-family: Google Sans Text, sans-serif !important; line-height: 1.15 !important; margin-top: 0px !important;\"><p data-path-to-node=\"7,0,0\" style=\"font-family: Google Sans Text, sans-serif !important; line-height: 1.15 !important; margin-top: 0px !important;\"><b data-path-to-node=\"7,0,0\" data-index-in-node=\"0\" style=\"font-family: Google Sans Text, sans-serif !important; line-height: 1.15 !important; margin-top: 0px !important;\">What it says:</b> There exists a function <span class=\"math-inline\" data-math=\"f\" data-index-in-node=\"38\" style=\"font-family: Google Sans Text, sans-serif !important; line-height: 1.15 !important; margin-top: 0px !important;\">$f$</span> from <span class=\"math-inline\" data-math=\"A\" data-index-in-node=\"45\" style=\"font-family: Google Sans Text, sans-serif !important; line-height: 1.15 !important; margin-top: 0px !important;\">$A$</span> to <span class=\"math-inline\" data-math=\"B\" data-index-in-node=\"50\" style=\"font-family: Google Sans Text, sans-serif !important; line-height: 1.15 !important; margin-top: 0px !important;\">$B$</span> such that distinct elements in the domain <span class=\"math-inline\" data-math=\"A\" data-index-in-node=\"94\" style=\"font-family: Google Sans Text, sans-serif !important; line-height: 1.15 !important; margin-top: 0px !important;\">$A$</span> map to distinct elements in the codomain <span class=\"math-inline\" data-math=\"B\" data-index-in-node=\"137\" style=\"font-family: Google Sans Text, sans-serif !important; line-height: 1.15 !important; margin-top: 0px !important;\">$B$</span>.</p></li><li style=\"font-family: Google Sans Text, sans-serif !important; line-height: 1.15 !important; margin-top: 0px !important;\"><p data-path-to-node=\"7,1,0\" style=\"font-family: Google Sans Text, sans-serif !important; line-height: 1.15 !important; margin-top: 0px !important;\"><b data-path-to-node=\"7,1,0\" data-index-in-node=\"0\" style=\"font-family: Google Sans Text, sans-serif !important; line-height: 1.15 !important; margin-top: 0px !important;\">Mathematical Meaning:</b> This is the formal definition of an <b data-path-to-node=\"7,1,0\" data-index-in-node=\"58\" style=\"font-family: Google Sans Text, sans-serif !important; line-height: 1.15 !important; margin-top: 0px !important;\">injective (one-to-one) function</b>.</p></li><li style=\"font-family: Google Sans Text, sans-serif !important; line-height: 1.15 !important; margin-top: 0px !important;\"><p data-path-to-node=\"7,2,0\" style=\"font-family: Google Sans Text, sans-serif !important; line-height: 1.15 !important; margin-top: 0px !important;\"><b data-path-to-node=\"7,2,0\" data-index-in-node=\"0\" style=\"font-family: Google Sans Text, sans-serif !important; line-height: 1.15 !important; margin-top: 0px !important;\">Cardinality Implication:</b> For an injective function to exist, the target set <span class=\"math-inline\" data-math=\"B\" data-index-in-node=\"76\" style=\"font-family: Google Sans Text, sans-serif !important; line-height: 1.15 !important; margin-top: 0px !important;\">$B$</span> must have at least as many elements as the starting set <span class=\"math-inline\" data-math=\"A\" data-index-in-node=\"134\" style=\"font-family: Google Sans Text, sans-serif !important; line-height: 1.15 !important; margin-top: 0px !important;\">$A$</span>. Otherwise, you would run out of unique targets (by the Pigeonhole Principle).</p><div data-path-to-node=\"7,2,1\" style=\"font-family: Google Sans Text, sans-serif !important; line-height: 1.15 !important; margin-top: 0px !important;\"><div class=\"math-block\" data-math=\"\\text{Therefore, } |A| \\le |B|\" style=\"font-family: Google Sans Text, sans-serif !important; line-height: 1.15 !important; margin-top: 0px !important;\">$$\\text{Therefore, } |A| \\le |B|$$</div></div></li></ul><h4 data-path-to-node=\"9\" style=\"font-family: Google Sans, sans-serif !important; line-height: 1.15 !important; margin-top: 0px !important;\"><b data-path-to-node=\"9\" data-index-in-node=\"0\" style=\"font-family: Google Sans Text, sans-serif !important; line-height: 1.15 !important; margin-top: 0px !important;\">Statement II: Surjection (Onto)</b></h4><div data-path-to-node=\"10\" style=\"font-family: Google Sans Text, sans-serif !important; line-height: 1.15 !important; margin-top: 0px !important;\"><div class=\"math-block\" data-math=\"\\mathbf{II}. \\quad \\exists f : A \\to B \\quad \\forall b \\in B \\quad \\exists a \\in A \\, (f(a) = b)\" style=\"font-family: Google Sans Text, sans-serif !important; line-height: 1.15 !important; margin-top: 0px !important;\">$$\\mathbf{II}. \\quad \\exists f : A \\to B \\quad \\forall b \\in B \\quad \\exists a \\in A \\, (f(a) = b)$$</div></div><ul data-path-to-node=\"11\" style=\"padding-inline-start: 32px; font-family: Google Sans Text, sans-serif !important; line-height: 1.15 !important; margin-top: 0px !important;\"><li style=\"font-family: Google Sans Text, sans-serif !important; line-height: 1.15 !important; margin-top: 0px !important;\"><p data-path-to-node=\"11,0,0\" style=\"font-family: Google Sans Text, sans-serif !important; line-height: 1.15 !important; margin-top: 0px !important;\"><b data-path-to-node=\"11,0,0\" data-index-in-node=\"0\" style=\"font-family: Google Sans Text, sans-serif !important; line-height: 1.15 !important; margin-top: 0px !important;\">What it says:</b> There exists a function <span class=\"math-inline\" data-math=\"f\" data-index-in-node=\"38\" style=\"font-family: Google Sans Text, sans-serif !important; line-height: 1.15 !important; margin-top: 0px !important;\">$f$</span> from <span class=\"math-inline\" data-math=\"A\" data-index-in-node=\"45\" style=\"font-family: Google Sans Text, sans-serif !important; line-height: 1.15 !important; margin-top: 0px !important;\">$A$</span> to <span class=\"math-inline\" data-math=\"B\" data-index-in-node=\"50\" style=\"font-family: Google Sans Text, sans-serif !important; line-height: 1.15 !important; margin-top: 0px !important;\">$B$</span> such that every single element <span class=\"math-inline\" data-math=\"b\" data-index-in-node=\"83\" style=\"font-family: Google Sans Text, sans-serif !important; line-height: 1.15 !important; margin-top: 0px !important;\">$b$</span> in the codomain <span class=\"math-inline\" data-math=\"B\" data-index-in-node=\"101\" style=\"font-family: Google Sans Text, sans-serif !important; line-height: 1.15 !important; margin-top: 0px !important;\">$B$</span> is mapped to by at least one element <span class=\"math-inline\" data-math=\"a\" data-index-in-node=\"140\" style=\"font-family: Google Sans Text, sans-serif !important; line-height: 1.15 !important; margin-top: 0px !important;\">$a$</span> in the domain <span class=\"math-inline\" data-math=\"A\" data-index-in-node=\"156\" style=\"font-family: Google Sans Text, sans-serif !important; line-height: 1.15 !important; margin-top: 0px !important;\">$A$</span>.</p></li><li style=\"font-family: Google Sans Text, sans-serif !important; line-height: 1.15 !important; margin-top: 0px !important;\"><p data-path-to-node=\"11,1,0\" style=\"font-family: Google Sans Text, sans-serif !important; line-height: 1.15 !important; margin-top: 0px !important;\"><b data-path-to-node=\"11,1,0\" data-index-in-node=\"0\" style=\"font-family: Google Sans Text, sans-serif !important; line-height: 1.15 !important; margin-top: 0px !important;\">Mathematical Meaning:</b> This is the formal definition of a <b data-path-to-node=\"11,1,0\" data-index-in-node=\"57\" style=\"font-family: Google Sans Text, sans-serif !important; line-height: 1.15 !important; margin-top: 0px !important;\">surjective (onto) function</b>.</p></li><li style=\"font-family: Google Sans Text, sans-serif !important; line-height: 1.15 !important; margin-top: 0px !important;\"><p data-path-to-node=\"11,2,0\" style=\"font-family: Google Sans Text, sans-serif !important; line-height: 1.15 !important; margin-top: 0px !important;\"><b data-path-to-node=\"11,2,0\" data-index-in-node=\"0\" style=\"font-family: Google Sans Text, sans-serif !important; line-height: 1.15 !important; margin-top: 0px !important;\">Cardinality Implication:</b> For a surjective function to exist, the starting set <span class=\"math-inline\" data-math=\"A\" data-index-in-node=\"78\" style=\"font-family: Google Sans Text, sans-serif !important; line-height: 1.15 !important; margin-top: 0px !important;\">$A$</span> must have at least as many elements as the target set <span class=\"math-inline\" data-math=\"B\" data-index-in-node=\"134\" style=\"font-family: Google Sans Text, sans-serif !important; line-height: 1.15 !important; margin-top: 0px !important;\">$B$</span> to cover every value in <span class=\"math-inline\" data-math=\"B\" data-index-in-node=\"160\" style=\"font-family: Google Sans Text, sans-serif !important; line-height: 1.15 !important; margin-top: 0px !important;\">$B$</span>.</p><div data-path-to-node=\"11,2,1\" style=\"font-family: Google Sans Text, sans-serif !important; line-height: 1.15 !important; margin-top: 0px !important;\"><div class=\"math-block\" data-math=\"\\text{Therefore, } |A| \\ge |B|\" style=\"font-family: Google Sans Text, sans-serif !important; line-height: 1.15 !important; margin-top: 0px !important;\">$$\\text{Therefore, } |A| \\ge |B|$$</div></div></li></ul><h4 data-path-to-node=\"13\" style=\"font-family: Google Sans, sans-serif !important; line-height: 1.15 !important; margin-top: 0px !important;\"><b data-path-to-node=\"13\" data-index-in-node=\"0\" style=\"font-family: Google Sans Text, sans-serif !important; line-height: 1.15 !important; margin-top: 0px !important;\">Statement III: Bijection (One-to-One and Onto)</b></h4><div data-path-to-node=\"14\" style=\"font-family: Google Sans Text, sans-serif !important; line-height: 1.15 !important; margin-top: 0px !important;\"><div class=\"math-block\" data-math=\"\\mathbf{III}. \\quad \\exists f : A \\to B \\quad \\forall b \\in B \\quad \\exists a \\in A \\, (f(a) = b \\land \\forall a' \\in A \\, (a \\neq a' \\to f(a') \\neq b))\" style=\"font-family: Google Sans Text, sans-serif !important; line-height: 1.15 !important; margin-top: 0px !important;\">$$\\mathbf{III}. \\quad \\exists f : A \\to B \\quad \\forall b \\in B \\quad \\exists a \\in A \\, (f(a) = b \\land \\forall a' \\in A \\, (a \\neq a' \\to f(a') \\neq b))$$</div></div><ul data-path-to-node=\"15\" style=\"padding-inline-start: 32px; font-family: Google Sans Text, sans-serif !important; line-height: 1.15 !important; margin-top: 0px !important;\"><li style=\"font-family: Google Sans Text, sans-serif !important; line-height: 1.15 !important; margin-top: 0px !important;\"><p data-path-to-node=\"15,0,0\" style=\"font-family: Google Sans Text, sans-serif !important; line-height: 1.15 !important; margin-top: 0px !important;\"><b data-path-to-node=\"15,0,0\" data-index-in-node=\"0\" style=\"font-family: Google Sans Text, sans-serif !important; line-height: 1.15 !important; margin-top: 0px !important;\">What it says:</b> This is a combination of both concepts. It states that for every element <span class=\"math-inline\" data-math=\"b \\in B\" data-index-in-node=\"87\" style=\"font-family: Google Sans Text, sans-serif !important; line-height: 1.15 !important; margin-top: 0px !important;\">$b \\in B$</span>, there exists an element <span class=\"math-inline\" data-math=\"a \\in A\" data-index-in-node=\"120\" style=\"font-family: Google Sans Text, sans-serif !important; line-height: 1.15 !important; margin-top: 0px !important;\">$a \\in A$</span> that maps to it (<span class=\"math-inline\" data-math=\"f(a) = b\" data-index-in-node=\"145\" style=\"font-family: Google Sans Text, sans-serif !important; line-height: 1.15 !important; margin-top: 0px !important;\">$f(a) = b$</span>), <b data-path-to-node=\"15,0,0\" data-index-in-node=\"156\" style=\"font-family: Google Sans Text, sans-serif !important; line-height: 1.15 !important; margin-top: 0px !important;\">and</b> any other distinct element <span class=\"math-inline\" data-math=\"a'\" data-index-in-node=\"187\" style=\"font-family: Google Sans Text, sans-serif !important; line-height: 1.15 !important; margin-top: 0px !important;\">$a'$</span> does <i data-path-to-node=\"15,0,0\" data-index-in-node=\"195\" style=\"font-family: Google Sans Text, sans-serif !important; line-height: 1.15 !important; margin-top: 0px !important;\">not</i> map to <span class=\"math-inline\" data-math=\"b\" data-index-in-node=\"206\" style=\"font-family: Google Sans Text, sans-serif !important; line-height: 1.15 !important; margin-top: 0px !important;\">$b$</span>. In plain English, every element in <span class=\"math-inline\" data-math=\"B\" data-index-in-node=\"244\" style=\"font-family: Google Sans Text, sans-serif !important; line-height: 1.15 !important; margin-top: 0px !important;\">$B$</span> has exactly one unique pre-image in <span class=\"math-inline\" data-math=\"A\" data-index-in-node=\"282\" style=\"font-family: Google Sans Text, sans-serif !important; line-height: 1.15 !important; margin-top: 0px !important;\">$A$</span>.</p></li><li style=\"font-family: Google Sans Text, sans-serif !important; line-height: 1.15 !important; margin-top: 0px !important;\"><p data-path-to-node=\"15,1,0\" style=\"font-family: Google Sans Text, sans-serif !important; line-height: 1.15 !important; margin-top: 0px !important;\"><b data-path-to-node=\"15,1,0\" data-index-in-node=\"0\" style=\"font-family: Google Sans Text, sans-serif !important; line-height: 1.15 !important; margin-top: 0px !important;\">Mathematical Meaning:</b> This is the definition of a <b data-path-to-node=\"15,1,0\" data-index-in-node=\"50\" style=\"font-family: Google Sans Text, sans-serif !important; line-height: 1.15 !important; margin-top: 0px !important;\">bijective function</b> (a perfect one-to-one pairing).</p></li><li style=\"font-family: Google Sans Text, sans-serif !important; line-height: 1.15 !important; margin-top: 0px !important;\"><p data-path-to-node=\"15,2,0\" style=\"font-family: Google Sans Text, sans-serif !important; line-height: 1.15 !important; margin-top: 0px !important;\"><b data-path-to-node=\"15,2,0\" data-index-in-node=\"0\" style=\"font-family: Google Sans Text, sans-serif !important; line-height: 1.15 !important; margin-top: 0px !important;\">Cardinality Implication:</b> For a perfect matching to exist between two sets, they must be exactly the same size.</p><div data-path-to-node=\"15,2,1\" style=\"font-family: Google Sans Text, sans-serif !important; line-height: 1.15 !important; margin-top: 0px !important;\"><div class=\"math-block\" data-math=\"\\text{Therefore, } |A| = |B|\" style=\"font-family: Google Sans Text, sans-serif !important; line-height: 1.15 !important; margin-top: 0px !important;\">$$\\text{Therefore, } |A| = |B|$$</div></div></li></ul><h3 data-path-to-node=\"17\" style=\"font-family: Google Sans, sans-serif !important; line-height: 1.15 !important; margin-top: 0px !important;\"><b data-path-to-node=\"17\" data-index-in-node=\"0\" style=\"font-family: Google Sans Text, sans-serif !important; line-height: 1.15 !important; margin-top: 0px !important;\">Conclusion</b></h3><p data-path-to-node=\"18\" style=\"font-family: Google Sans Text, sans-serif !important; line-height: 1.15 !important; margin-top: 0px !important;\">Matching our results with the choices:</p><ul data-path-to-node=\"19\" style=\"padding-inline-start: 32px; font-family: Google Sans Text, sans-serif !important; line-height: 1.15 !important; margin-top: 0px !important;\"><li style=\"font-family: Google Sans Text, sans-serif !important; line-height: 1.15 !important; margin-top: 0px !important;\"><p data-path-to-node=\"19,0,0\" style=\"font-family: Google Sans Text, sans-serif !important; line-height: 1.15 !important; margin-top: 0px !important;\"><span class=\"math-inline\" data-math=\"\\mathbf{I}: |A| \\le |B|\" data-index-in-node=\"0\" style=\"font-family: Google Sans Text, sans-serif !important; line-height: 1.15 !important; margin-top: 0px !important;\">$\\mathbf{I}: |A| \\le |B|$</span></p></li><li style=\"font-family: Google Sans Text, sans-serif !important; line-height: 1.15 !important; margin-top: 0px !important;\"><p data-path-to-node=\"19,1,0\" style=\"font-family: Google Sans Text, sans-serif !important; line-height: 1.15 !important; margin-top: 0px !important;\"><span class=\"math-inline\" data-math=\"\\mathbf{II}: |A| \\ge |B|\" data-index-in-node=\"0\" style=\"font-family: Google Sans Text, sans-serif !important; line-height: 1.15 !important; margin-top: 0px !important;\">$\\mathbf{II}: |A| \\ge |B|$</span></p></li><li style=\"font-family: Google Sans Text, sans-serif !important; line-height: 1.15 !important; margin-top: 0px !important;\"><p data-path-to-node=\"19,2,0\" style=\"font-family: Google Sans Text, sans-serif !important; line-height: 1.15 !important; margin-top: 0px !important;\"><span class=\"math-inline\" data-math=\"\\mathbf{III}: |A| = |B|\" data-index-in-node=\"0\" style=\"font-family: Google Sans Text, sans-serif !important; line-height: 1.15 !important; margin-top: 0px !important;\">$\\mathbf{III}: |A| = |B|$</span></p></li></ul><p data-path-to-node=\"20\" style=\"font-family: Google Sans Text, sans-serif !important; line-height: 1.15 !important; margin-top: 0px !important;\">This matches <b data-path-to-node=\"20\" data-index-in-node=\"13\" style=\"font-family: Google Sans Text, sans-serif !important; line-height: 1.15 !important; margin-top: 0px !important;\">Option B</b>.</p></div></div></section><section><h4>Source solution 2 <small>(score: 0)</small></h4><a name=\"535551\"></a><div itemprop=\"text\"><p>Statement $\\text{I}$ is definition of injective function<br>So, if $f : A\\to B$ is injection then $|A| \\le |B|$</p><p>Statement $\\text{II}$ is definition of surjective function<br>So, if $f : A\\to B$ is surjection then $|A| \\ge |B|$</p><p>Statement $\\text{III}$ is definition of bijective function<br>So, if $f : A\\to B$ is bijection then $|A| = |B|$</p><p>Learn about injective, surjective and bijective functions:</p><div style=\"text-align: center;\"><p><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer nofollow\" style=\"display: inline-block; background-color: rgb(0, 140, 186); color: white; padding: 10px 20px; text-align: center; text-decoration: none; font-size: 16px; border-radius: 6px; border-width: medium; border-style: none; border-color: currentcolor; border-image: initial; font-family: sans-serif; --darkreader-inline-bgcolor: var(--darkreader-background-008cba, #007095); --darkreader-inline-color: var(--darkreader-text-ffffff, #e8e6e3);\" data-darkreader-inline-bgcolor=\"\" data-darkreader-inline-color=\"\" href=\"https://youtu.be/UWuIllbQuP0?list=PLIPZ2_p3RNHgXosiQv-gL1PvJkcHokW1p&amp;t=900\">Watch here!</a> &nbsp;&nbsp;</p></div></div></section></div><p><a href=\"https://gateoverflow.in/532607\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Official discussion page</a></p>","keyMissing":true},{"n":4,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"","html":"<p>Let $\\mathbb{R}$ denote the set of real numbers, $\\mathbb{Z}$ denote the set of integers, and let $U=\\mathcal{P}(\\mathbb{R})$ be the power set of $\\mathbb{R}$ (meaning the elements of $U$ are subsets of $\\mathbb{R}$).</p><p>Consider the following two statements:</p><p>$\\mathbf{Statement\\ I}$: For all subsets $X$ and $Y$ in $U$, if $X$ and $Y$ have the same cardinality $(|X|=|Y|)$, then $X$ and $Y$ must be the identical set $(X=Y)$.</p><p>$\\mathbf{Statement\\ II}$: There exist two subsets $A$ and $B$ in $U$ such that both $A$ and $B$ contain the set of integers as a subset $(\\mathbb{Z}\\subseteq A \\text{ and } \\mathbb{Z}\\subseteq B)$, yet $A$ and $B$ have strictly different cardinalities $(|A|\\neq|B|)$.</p><p>Which of the following is correct ?</p>","paragraph":null,"options":[{"key":"A","html":"<p>Both Statement $\\mathbf{\\ I}$ and Statement&nbsp;$\\mathbf{\\ II}$&nbsp;are false.<br>&nbsp;</p>"},{"key":"B","html":"<p>Statement $\\mathbf{\\ I}$ is true, but Statement $\\mathbf{\\ II}$&nbsp;is false.<br>&nbsp;</p>"},{"key":"C","html":"<p>Statement $\\mathbf{\\ I}$ is false, but Statement $\\mathbf{\\ II}$ is true.<br>&nbsp;</p>"},{"key":"D","html":"<p>Both Statement $\\mathbf{\\ I}$ and Statement $\\mathbf{\\ II}$&nbsp;are true.</p>"}],"answer":[],"natRange":null,"answerText":"","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0.3333},"solution":"<p><strong>Answer:</strong> Not available</p><div class=\"source-solutions\" data-answer-source=\"solution_only\"><section><h4>Source solution 1 <small>(score: 0)</small></h4><a name=\"535553\"></a><div itemprop=\"text\">Statement $\\text{I}:$ For all subsets $X$ and $Y$ in $U$, if $X$ and $Y$ have the same cardinality $(|X|=|Y|)$, then $X$ and $Y$ must be the identical set $(X=Y)$.<br>\n<br>\nFalse. Take $ X = \\{1,2,3\\}$ and $ Y = \\{4,5,6\\}$<br>\n&nbsp;<br>\n<br>\nStatement $\\text{II}:$ There exist two subsets $A$ and $B$ in $U$ such that both $A$ and $B$ contain the set of integers as a subset $(\\mathbb{Z}\\subseteq A \\text{ and } \\mathbb{Z}\\subseteq B)$, yet $A$ and $B$ have strictly different cardinalities $(|A|\\neq|B|)$.<br>\n<br>\nTrue. Take $A = \\mathbb{Z}$ and $B = \\mathbb{R}$</div></section></div><p><a href=\"https://gateoverflow.in/532606\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Official discussion page</a></p>","keyMissing":true},{"n":5,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"","html":"<p>Let $\\mathbb{N}$ denote the set of natural numbers and $\\mathcal{P}(\\mathbb{N})$ denote its power set. Which of the following functions successfully serves as a valid example to establish the inequality $|\\mathcal{P}(\\mathbb{N})| \\leq|\\mathbb{N}|$ ?</p>","paragraph":null,"options":[{"key":"A","html":"<p>$g_{A}: \\mathcal{P}(\\mathbb{N}) \\rightarrow \\mathbb{N} \\quad$ where $\\quad g_{A}(x)= \\begin{cases}0 \\& \\text { if } x=\\emptyset \\\\ 1 \\& \\text { otherwise }\\end{cases}$<br>&nbsp;</p>"},{"key":"B","html":"<p>$g_{B}: \\mathcal{P}(\\mathbb{N}) \\rightarrow \\mathbb{N} \\quad$ where $\\quad g_{B}(x)=20$<br>&nbsp;</p>"},{"key":"C","html":"<p>$g_{C}: \\mathcal{P}(\\mathbb{N}) \\rightarrow \\mathbb{N} \\quad$ where $\\quad g_{C}(x)= \\begin{cases}\\min (x) \\& \\text { if } x \\neq \\emptyset \\\\ 0 \\& \\text { otherwise }\\end{cases}$<br>&nbsp;</p>"},{"key":"D","html":"<p>None of the above, because no such function can exist.</p>"}],"answer":[],"natRange":null,"answerText":"","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0.3333},"solution":"<p><strong>Answer:</strong> Not available</p><div class=\"source-solutions\" data-answer-source=\"solution_only\"><section><h4>Source solution 1 <small>(score: 0)</small></h4><a name=\"535556\"></a><div itemprop=\"text\"><p>To prove $A \\le B$, we need to show that there exist injective function $f : A \\to B$.</p><p>But $|\\mathcal{P}(\\mathbb{N})| \\leq|\\mathbb{N}|$ is not possible. Why?</p><p>Because by <strong data-start=\"970\" data-end=\"990\">Cantor's theorem</strong>, the power set of any set always has strictly larger cardinality than the set itself.</p><p>So, no such injective function from power set to set itself exist.</p><div style=\"text-align: center;\"><p><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer nofollow\" style=\"display: inline-block; background-color: rgb(0, 140, 186); color: white; padding: 10px 20px; text-align: center; text-decoration: none; font-size: 16px; border-radius: 6px; border-width: medium; border-style: none; border-color: currentcolor; border-image: initial; font-family: sans-serif; --darkreader-inline-bgcolor: var(--darkreader-background-008cba, #007095); --darkreader-inline-color: var(--darkreader-text-ffffff, #e8e6e3);\" data-darkreader-inline-bgcolor=\"\" data-darkreader-inline-color=\"\" href=\"https://youtu.be/loHylTwhcrU?list=PLIPZ2_p3RNHgXosiQv-gL1PvJkcHokW1p&amp;t=840\">Watch here!</a> &nbsp;&nbsp;</p></div></div></section></div><p><a href=\"https://gateoverflow.in/532605\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Official discussion page</a></p>","keyMissing":true},{"n":6,"type":"MSQ","section":"Technical","tags":"","html":"<p>Let $\\mathbb{N}$ denote the set of all natural numbers $\\{1,2,3, \\ldots\\}$. Which of the following statements is/are true? (Select all that apply)</p>","paragraph":null,"options":[{"key":"A","html":"<p>If $A_{1}, A_{2}, \\ldots, A_{n}$ is a finite collection of countable sets, then their Cartesian product $A_{1} \\times A_{2} \\times \\cdots \\times A_{n}$ is also countable.<br>&nbsp;</p>"},{"key":"B","html":"<p>If there exists an injective function $f: A \\rightarrow \\mathbb{N}$, then the set $A$ must be countable.<br>&nbsp;</p>"},{"key":"C","html":"<p>If there exists a surjective function $f: \\mathbb{N} \\rightarrow A$, then the set $A$ must be countable.<br>&nbsp;</p>"},{"key":"D","html":"<p>If $A_{n}$ is a countable set for every $n \\in \\mathbb{N}$, then their countable union $A=\\bigcup_{n=1}^{\\infty} A_{n}$ is also countable.</p>"}],"answer":[],"natRange":null,"answerText":"","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0},"solution":"<p><strong>Answer:</strong> Not available</p><div class=\"source-solutions\" data-answer-source=\"solution_only\"><section><h4>Source solution 1 <small>(score: 0)</small></h4><a name=\"535559\"></a><div itemprop=\"text\"><p>A.&nbsp;If $A_{1}, A_{2}, \\ldots, A_{n}$ is a finite collection of countable sets, then their Cartesian product $A_{1} \\times A_{2} \\times \\cdots \\times A_{n}$ is also countable. ${\\color{blue}{\\text{True}}}$</p><div style=\"text-align: center;\"><p><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer nofollow\" style=\"display: inline-block; background-color: rgb(0, 140, 186); color: white; padding: 10px 20px; text-align: center; text-decoration: none; font-size: 16px; border-radius: 6px; border-width: medium; border-style: none; border-color: currentcolor; border-image: initial; font-family: sans-serif; --darkreader-inline-bgcolor: var(--darkreader-background-008cba, #007095); --darkreader-inline-color: var(--darkreader-text-ffffff, #e8e6e3);\" data-darkreader-inline-bgcolor=\"\" data-darkreader-inline-color=\"\" href=\"https://youtu.be/w8L1pBaD1oo?list=PLIPZ2_p3RNHgXosiQv-gL1PvJkcHokW1p&amp;t=4382\">Watch here!</a>&nbsp; &nbsp;</p></div><p><br>B.&nbsp;If there exists an injective function $f: A \\rightarrow \\mathbb{N}$, then the set $A$ must be countable.&nbsp;${\\color{blue}{\\text{True}}}$&nbsp;</p><div style=\"text-align: center;\"><p><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer nofollow\" style=\"display: inline-block; background-color: rgb(0, 140, 186); color: white; padding: 10px 20px; text-align: center; text-decoration: none; font-size: 16px; border-radius: 6px; border-width: medium; border-style: none; border-color: currentcolor; border-image: initial; font-family: sans-serif; --darkreader-inline-bgcolor: var(--darkreader-background-008cba, #007095); --darkreader-inline-color: var(--darkreader-text-ffffff, #e8e6e3);\" data-darkreader-inline-bgcolor=\"\" data-darkreader-inline-color=\"\" href=\"https://youtu.be/P7O3xqnsuBA?list=PLIPZ2_p3RNHgXosiQv-gL1PvJkcHokW1p&amp;t=4910\">Watch here!</a>&nbsp; &nbsp;</p></div><p><br>C.&nbsp;If there exists a surjective function $f: \\mathbb{N} \\rightarrow A$, then the set $A$ must be countable.&nbsp;${\\color{blue}{\\text{True}}}$&nbsp;</p><p>Since $f$ is surjective, every element of $A$ appears somewhere in the sequence $f(1),f(2),f(3), \\dots$<br>So the elements of $A$ can be <strong data-start=\"436\" data-end=\"446\">listed</strong>&nbsp;with indexed by natural numbers. (possibly with repetitions).<br>Hence, $A$ is either finite or countable infinite. In both the cases, $A$ is countable.<br>&nbsp;</p><p>D.&nbsp;If $A_{n}$ is a countable set for every $n \\in \\mathbb{N}$, then their countable union $A=\\bigcup_{n=1}^{\\infty} A_{n}$ is also countable.&nbsp;${\\color{blue}{\\text{True}}}$</p><div style=\"text-align: center;\"><p><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer nofollow\" style=\"display: inline-block; background-color: rgb(0, 140, 186); color: white; padding: 10px 20px; text-align: center; text-decoration: none; font-size: 16px; border-radius: 6px; border-width: medium; border-style: none; border-color: currentcolor; border-image: initial; font-family: sans-serif; --darkreader-inline-bgcolor: var(--darkreader-background-008cba, #007095); --darkreader-inline-color: var(--darkreader-text-ffffff, #e8e6e3);\" data-darkreader-inline-bgcolor=\"\" data-darkreader-inline-color=\"\" href=\"https://youtu.be/w8L1pBaD1oo?t=3052\">Watch here!</a> &nbsp;&nbsp;</p></div></div></section></div><p><a href=\"https://gateoverflow.in/532604\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Official discussion page</a></p>","keyMissing":true},{"n":7,"type":"MSQ","section":"Technical","tags":"","html":"<p>Let $A$ and $B$ be two non-empty sets, and let $|A|$ and $|B|$ denote their respective cardinalities. We wish to prove that the two sets have same cardinality, i.e., $|A|=|B|$. Which of the following conditions, considered independently, is/are sufficient to establish the claim? (Select all that apply)</p>","paragraph":null,"options":[{"key":"A","html":"<p>Proving that there exists a bijective function $f: A \\rightarrow B$.<br>&nbsp;</p>"},{"key":"B","html":"<p>Proving that there exists a bijective function $f: B \\rightarrow A$.<br>&nbsp;</p>"},{"key":"C","html":"<p>Proving that there exist two injective (one-to-one) functions $f_{1}$ : $A \\rightarrow B$ and $f_{2}: B \\rightarrow A$.<br>&nbsp;</p>"},{"key":"D","html":"<p>Proving that there exist two surjective (onto) functions $f_{1}: A \\rightarrow B$ and $f_{2}: B \\rightarrow A$.</p>"}],"answer":[],"natRange":null,"answerText":"","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0},"solution":"<p><strong>Answer:</strong> Not available</p><div class=\"source-solutions\" data-answer-source=\"solution_only\"><section><h4>Source solution 1 <small>(score: 0)</small></h4><a name=\"535560\"></a><div itemprop=\"text\">ALL four are correct ways of proving $|A| =|B|$</div></section></div><p><a href=\"https://gateoverflow.in/532603\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Official discussion page</a></p>","keyMissing":true},{"n":8,"type":"MSQ","section":"Technical","tags":"","html":"<p>Cantor's diagonalization argument constructs, for any given function $f: \\mathbb{N} \\rightarrow \\mathcal{P}(\\mathbb{N})$, a associated diagonal set $D_{f} \\subseteq \\mathbb{N}$ defined explicitly as:<br>$$<br>D_{f}=\\{x \\in \\mathbb{N} \\mid x \\notin f(x)\\}<br>$$<br>Let $\\mathbb{N}=\\{0,1,2,3, \\ldots\\}$ denote the set of natural numbers. Consider the following two functions from $\\mathbb{N}$ to its power set $\\mathcal{P}(\\mathbb{N})$ :<br><br>$f_{1}(x)=\\{y \\in \\mathbb{N} \\mid y$ has the same parity as $x\\} \\quad$ (i.e., both are even or both are odd)<br><br>$f_{2}(x)=\\{y \\in \\mathbb{N} \\mid y&gt;x\\}$<br><br>Which of the following statements regarding their diagonal sets $D_{f_{1}}$ and $D_{f_{2}}$ is/are true? (Select all that apply)</p>","paragraph":null,"options":[{"key":"A","html":"<p>The diagonal set $D_{f_{1}}$ is empty (i.e., $D_{f_{1}}=\\emptyset$ ).<br>&nbsp;</p>"},{"key":"B","html":"<p>$1 \\in D_{f_{2}}$.<br>&nbsp;</p>"},{"key":"C","html":"<p>The diagonal set $D_{f_{2}}$ is countably infinite.<br>&nbsp;</p>"},{"key":"D","html":"<p>The diagonal set $D_{f_{2}}$ contains all natural numbers except 0 .</p>"}],"answer":["A","B","C"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"A, B, C","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0},"solution":"<p><strong>Answer:</strong> A, B, C</p><div class=\"source-solutions\" data-answer-source=\"inferred\"><section><h4>Source solution 1 <small>(score: 0)</small></h4><a name=\"535528\"></a><div itemprop=\"text\"><div class=\"markdown markdown-main-panel stronger enable-updated-hr-color\" style=\"--animation-duration: 400ms; --fade-animation-function: ease-out; font-family: Google Sans Text, sans-serif !important; line-height: 1.15 !important; margin-top: 0px !important;\" id=\"model-response-message-contentr_caffd52ac9a59348\" aria-live=\"polite\" aria-busy=\"false\" dir=\"ltr\"><h3 data-path-to-node=\"3\" style=\"font-family: Google Sans, sans-serif !important; line-height: 1.15 !important; margin-top: 0px !important;\"><b data-path-to-node=\"3\" data-index-in-node=\"0\" style=\"font-family: Google Sans Text, sans-serif !important; line-height: 1.15 !important; margin-top: 0px !important;\">Diagonal Sets of Functions</b></h3><p data-path-to-node=\"4\" style=\"font-family: Google Sans Text, sans-serif !important; line-height: 1.15 !important; margin-top: 0px !important;\">The diagonal set of a function <span class=\"math-inline\" data-math=\"f\" data-index-in-node=\"31\" style=\"font-family: Google Sans Text, sans-serif !important; line-height: 1.15 !important; margin-top: 0px !important;\">$f$</span> is defined as:</p><div data-path-to-node=\"5\" style=\"font-family: Google Sans Text, sans-serif !important; line-height: 1.15 !important; margin-top: 0px !important;\"><div class=\"math-block\" data-math=\"D_f = \\{x \\in \\mathbb{N} \\mid x \\notin f(x)\\}\" style=\"font-family: Google Sans Text, sans-serif !important; line-height: 1.15 !important; margin-top: 0px !important;\">$$D_f = \\{x \\in \\mathbb{N} \\mid x \\notin f(x)\\}$$</div></div><h4 data-path-to-node=\"6\" style=\"font-family: Google Sans, sans-serif !important; line-height: 1.15 !important; margin-top: 0px !important;\"><b data-path-to-node=\"6\" data-index-in-node=\"0\" style=\"font-family: Google Sans Text, sans-serif !important; line-height: 1.15 !important; margin-top: 0px !important;\">Analysis of <span class=\"math-inline\" data-math=\"f_1\" data-index-in-node=\"12\" style=\"font-family: Google Sans Text, sans-serif !important; line-height: 1.15 !important; margin-top: 0px !important;\">$f_1$</span></b></h4><p data-path-to-node=\"7\" style=\"font-family: Google Sans Text, sans-serif !important; line-height: 1.15 !important; margin-top: 0px !important;\">The function <span class=\"math-inline\" data-math=\"f_1\" data-index-in-node=\"13\" style=\"font-family: Google Sans Text, sans-serif !important; line-height: 1.15 !important; margin-top: 0px !important;\">$f_1$</span> maps each number to the set of all natural numbers having the same parity (even or odd) as that number:</p><div data-path-to-node=\"8\" style=\"font-family: Google Sans Text, sans-serif !important; line-height: 1.15 !important; margin-top: 0px !important;\"><div class=\"math-block\" data-math=\"f_1(x) = \\{y \\in \\mathbb{N} \\mid y \\equiv x \\pmod 2\\}\" style=\"font-family: Google Sans Text, sans-serif !important; line-height: 1.15 !important; margin-top: 0px !important;\">$$f_1(x) = \\{y \\in \\mathbb{N} \\mid y \\equiv x \\pmod 2\\}$$</div></div><ul data-path-to-node=\"9\" style=\"padding-inline-start: 32px; font-family: Google Sans Text, sans-serif !important; line-height: 1.15 !important; margin-top: 0px !important;\"><li style=\"font-family: Google Sans Text, sans-serif !important; line-height: 1.15 !important; margin-top: 0px !important;\"><p data-path-to-node=\"9,0,0\" style=\"font-family: Google Sans Text, sans-serif !important; line-height: 1.15 !important; margin-top: 0px !important;\">If <span class=\"math-inline\" data-math=\"x\" data-index-in-node=\"3\" style=\"font-family: Google Sans Text, sans-serif !important; line-height: 1.15 !important; margin-top: 0px !important;\">$x$</span> is even, then <span class=\"math-inline\" data-math=\"f_1(x)\" data-index-in-node=\"19\" style=\"font-family: Google Sans Text, sans-serif !important; line-height: 1.15 !important; margin-top: 0px !important;\">$f_1(x)$</span> contains all even numbers.</p></li><li style=\"font-family: Google Sans Text, sans-serif !important; line-height: 1.15 !important; margin-top: 0px !important;\"><p data-path-to-node=\"9,1,0\" style=\"font-family: Google Sans Text, sans-serif !important; line-height: 1.15 !important; margin-top: 0px !important;\">If <span class=\"math-inline\" data-math=\"x\" data-index-in-node=\"3\" style=\"font-family: Google Sans Text, sans-serif !important; line-height: 1.15 !important; margin-top: 0px !important;\">$x$</span> is odd, then <span class=\"math-inline\" data-math=\"f_1(x)\" data-index-in-node=\"18\" style=\"font-family: Google Sans Text, sans-serif !important; line-height: 1.15 !important; margin-top: 0px !important;\">$f_1(x)$</span> contains all odd numbers.</p></li></ul><p data-path-to-node=\"10\" style=\"font-family: Google Sans Text, sans-serif !important; line-height: 1.15 !important; margin-top: 0px !important;\">Since every number has the same parity as itself, we always have <span class=\"math-inline\" data-math=\"x \\in f_1(x)\" data-index-in-node=\"65\" style=\"font-family: Google Sans Text, sans-serif !important; line-height: 1.15 !important; margin-top: 0px !important;\">$x \\in f_1(x)$</span>. Therefore, there is no element <span class=\"math-inline\" data-math=\"x\" data-index-in-node=\"110\" style=\"font-family: Google Sans Text, sans-serif !important; line-height: 1.15 !important; margin-top: 0px !important;\">$x$</span> such that <span class=\"math-inline\" data-math=\"x \\notin f_1(x)\" data-index-in-node=\"122\" style=\"font-family: Google Sans Text, sans-serif !important; line-height: 1.15 !important; margin-top: 0px !important;\">$x \\notin f_1(x)$</span>.</p><p data-path-to-node=\"11\" style=\"font-family: Google Sans Text, sans-serif !important; line-height: 1.15 !important; margin-top: 0px !important;\">Hence, the diagonal set is empty:</p><div data-path-to-node=\"12\" style=\"font-family: Google Sans Text, sans-serif !important; line-height: 1.15 !important; margin-top: 0px !important;\"><div class=\"math-block\" data-math=\"D_{f_1} = \\varnothing\" style=\"font-family: Google Sans Text, sans-serif !important; line-height: 1.15 !important; margin-top: 0px !important;\">$$D_{f_1} = \\varnothing$$</div></div><p data-path-to-node=\"13\" style=\"font-family: Google Sans Text, sans-serif !important; line-height: 1.15 !important; margin-top: 0px !important;\">So, <b data-path-to-node=\"13\" data-index-in-node=\"4\" style=\"font-family: Google Sans Text, sans-serif !important; line-height: 1.15 !important; margin-top: 0px !important;\">Statement A is true</b>.</p><h4 data-path-to-node=\"15\" style=\"font-family: Google Sans, sans-serif !important; line-height: 1.15 !important; margin-top: 0px !important;\"><b data-path-to-node=\"15\" data-index-in-node=\"0\" style=\"font-family: Google Sans Text, sans-serif !important; line-height: 1.15 !important; margin-top: 0px !important;\">Analysis of <span class=\"math-inline\" data-math=\"f_2\" data-index-in-node=\"12\" style=\"font-family: Google Sans Text, sans-serif !important; line-height: 1.15 !important; margin-top: 0px !important;\">f2</span></b></h4><p data-path-to-node=\"16\" style=\"font-family: Google Sans Text, sans-serif !important; line-height: 1.15 !important; margin-top: 0px !important;\">The function <span class=\"math-inline\" data-math=\"f_2\" data-index-in-node=\"13\" style=\"font-family: Google Sans Text, sans-serif !important; line-height: 1.15 !important; margin-top: 0px !important;\">$f_2$</span> maps <span class=\"math-inline\" data-math=\"x\" data-index-in-node=\"22\" style=\"font-family: Google Sans Text, sans-serif !important; line-height: 1.15 !important; margin-top: 0px !important;\">$x$</span> to the set of all natural numbers strictly greater than <span class=\"math-inline\" data-math=\"x\" data-index-in-node=\"80\" style=\"font-family: Google Sans Text, sans-serif !important; line-height: 1.15 !important; margin-top: 0px !important;\">$x$</span>:</p><div data-path-to-node=\"17\" style=\"font-family: Google Sans Text, sans-serif !important; line-height: 1.15 !important; margin-top: 0px !important;\"><div class=\"math-block\" data-math=\"f_2(x) = \\{y \\in \\mathbb{N} \\mid y &gt; x\\}\" style=\"font-family: Google Sans Text, sans-serif !important; line-height: 1.15 !important; margin-top: 0px !important;\">$$f_2(x) = \\{y \\in \\mathbb{N} \\mid y &gt; x\\}$$</div></div><p data-path-to-node=\"18\" style=\"font-family: Google Sans Text, sans-serif !important; line-height: 1.15 !important; margin-top: 0px !important;\">A number is never greater than itself (<span class=\"math-inline\" data-math=\"x \\ngtr x\" data-index-in-node=\"39\" style=\"font-family: Google Sans Text, sans-serif !important; line-height: 1.15 !important; margin-top: 0px !important;\">$x \\ngtr x$</span>), so <span class=\"math-inline\" data-math=\"x \\notin f_2(x)\" data-index-in-node=\"54\" style=\"font-family: Google Sans Text, sans-serif !important; line-height: 1.15 !important; margin-top: 0px !important;\">$x \\notin f_2(x)$</span> holds true for every <span class=\"math-inline\" data-math=\"x \\in \\mathbb{N}\" data-index-in-node=\"91\" style=\"font-family: Google Sans Text, sans-serif !important; line-height: 1.15 !important; margin-top: 0px !important;\">$x \\in \\mathbb{N}$</span>.</p><p data-path-to-node=\"19\" style=\"font-family: Google Sans Text, sans-serif !important; line-height: 1.15 !important; margin-top: 0px !important;\">This means every single natural number satisfies the condition to belong to the diagonal set:</p><div data-path-to-node=\"20\" style=\"font-family: Google Sans Text, sans-serif !important; line-height: 1.15 !important; margin-top: 0px !important;\"><div class=\"math-block\" data-math=\"D_{f_2} = \\mathbb{N}\" style=\"font-family: Google Sans Text, sans-serif !important; line-height: 1.15 !important; margin-top: 0px !important;\">$$D_{f_2} = \\mathbb{N}$$</div></div><p data-path-to-node=\"21\" style=\"font-family: Google Sans Text, sans-serif !important; line-height: 1.15 !important; margin-top: 0px !important;\">From this, we can conclude:</p><ul data-path-to-node=\"22\" style=\"padding-inline-start: 32px; font-family: Google Sans Text, sans-serif !important; line-height: 1.15 !important; margin-top: 0px !important;\"><li style=\"font-family: Google Sans Text, sans-serif !important; line-height: 1.15 !important; margin-top: 0px !important;\"><p data-path-to-node=\"22,0,0\" style=\"font-family: Google Sans Text, sans-serif !important; line-height: 1.15 !important; margin-top: 0px !important;\"><span class=\"math-inline\" data-math=\"1 \\in D_{f_2}\" data-index-in-node=\"0\" style=\"font-family: Google Sans Text, sans-serif !important; line-height: 1.15 !important; margin-top: 0px !important;\">$1 \\in D_{f_2}$</span>, so <b data-path-to-node=\"22,0,0\" data-index-in-node=\"18\" style=\"font-family: Google Sans Text, sans-serif !important; line-height: 1.15 !important; margin-top: 0px !important;\">Statement B is true</b>.</p></li><li style=\"font-family: Google Sans Text, sans-serif !important; line-height: 1.15 !important; margin-top: 0px !important;\"><p data-path-to-node=\"22,1,0\" style=\"font-family: Google Sans Text, sans-serif !important; line-height: 1.15 !important; margin-top: 0px !important;\"><span class=\"math-inline\" data-math=\"D_{f_2} = \\mathbb{N}\" data-index-in-node=\"0\" style=\"font-family: Google Sans Text, sans-serif !important; line-height: 1.15 !important; margin-top: 0px !important;\">$D_{f_2} = \\mathbb{N}$</span>, which is countably infinite, so <b data-path-to-node=\"22,1,0\" data-index-in-node=\"54\" style=\"font-family: Google Sans Text, sans-serif !important; line-height: 1.15 !important; margin-top: 0px !important;\">Statement C is true</b>.</p></li><li style=\"font-family: Google Sans Text, sans-serif !important; line-height: 1.15 !important; margin-top: 0px !important;\"><p data-path-to-node=\"22,2,0\" style=\"font-family: Google Sans Text, sans-serif !important; line-height: 1.15 !important; margin-top: 0px !important;\"><b data-path-to-node=\"22,2,0\" data-index-in-node=\"0\" style=\"font-family: Google Sans Text, sans-serif !important; line-height: 1.15 !important; margin-top: 0px !important;\">Statement D is false</b> because <span class=\"math-inline\" data-math=\"0\" data-index-in-node=\"29\" style=\"font-family: Google Sans Text, sans-serif !important; line-height: 1.15 !important; margin-top: 0px !important;\">$0$</span> is also in <span class=\"math-inline\" data-math=\"D_{f_2}\" data-index-in-node=\"42\" style=\"font-family: Google Sans Text, sans-serif !important; line-height: 1.15 !important; margin-top: 0px !important;\">$D_{f_2}$</span> (since <span class=\"math-inline\" data-math=\"0 \\ngtr 0\" data-index-in-node=\"57\" style=\"font-family: Google Sans Text, sans-serif !important; line-height: 1.15 !important; margin-top: 0px !important;\">$0 \\ngtr 0$</span>). In fact, <span class=\"math-inline\" data-math=\"D_{f_2}\" data-index-in-node=\"78\" style=\"font-family: Google Sans Text, sans-serif !important; line-height: 1.15 !important; margin-top: 0px !important;\">$D_{f_2}$</span> contains all natural numbers, not all except <span class=\"math-inline\" data-math=\"0\" data-index-in-node=\"131\" style=\"font-family: Google Sans Text, sans-serif !important; line-height: 1.15 !important; margin-top: 0px !important;\">$0$</span>.</p></li></ul><h3 data-path-to-node=\"24\" style=\"font-family: Google Sans, sans-serif !important; line-height: 1.15 !important; margin-top: 0px !important;\"><b data-path-to-node=\"24\" data-index-in-node=\"0\" style=\"font-family: Google Sans Text, sans-serif !important; line-height: 1.15 !important; margin-top: 0px !important;\">Conclusion</b></h3><p data-path-to-node=\"25\" style=\"font-family: Google Sans Text, sans-serif !important; line-height: 1.15 !important; margin-top: 0px !important;\">Therefore, the correct answers are:</p><div data-path-to-node=\"26\" style=\"font-family: Google Sans Text, sans-serif !important; line-height: 1.15 !important; margin-top: 0px !important;\"><div class=\"math-block\" data-math=\"\\boxed{\\text{A, B, and C}}\" style=\"font-family: Google Sans Text, sans-serif !important; line-height: 1.15 !important; margin-top: 0px !important;\">$$\\boxed{\\text{A, B, and C}}$$</div></div><p data-path-to-node=\"27\" style=\"font-family: Google Sans Text, sans-serif !important; line-height: 1.15 !important; margin-top: 0px !important;\"><b data-path-to-node=\"27\" data-index-in-node=\"0\" style=\"font-family: Google Sans Text, sans-serif !important; line-height: 1.15 !important; margin-top: 0px !important;\">Key Observation:</b> For <span class=\"math-inline\" data-math=\"f_1\" data-index-in-node=\"21\" style=\"font-family: Google Sans Text, sans-serif !important; line-height: 1.15 !important; margin-top: 0px !important;\">$f_1$</span>, every element belongs to its own image, making <span class=\"math-inline\" data-math=\"D_{f_1}\" data-index-in-node=\"73\" style=\"font-family: Google Sans Text, sans-serif !important; line-height: 1.15 !important; margin-top: 0px !important;\">$D_{f_1}$</span> empty. For <span class=\"math-inline\" data-math=\"f_2\" data-index-in-node=\"92\" style=\"font-family: Google Sans Text, sans-serif !important; line-height: 1.15 !important; margin-top: 0px !important;\">$f_2$</span>, no element belongs to its own image, making <span class=\"math-inline\" data-math=\"D_{f_2}\" data-index-in-node=\"141\" style=\"font-family: Google Sans Text, sans-serif !important; line-height: 1.15 !important; margin-top: 0px !important;\">$D_{f_2}$</span> equal to the entire set of natural numbers <span class=\"math-inline\" data-math=\"\\mathbb{N}\" data-index-in-node=\"192\" style=\"font-family: Google Sans Text, sans-serif !important; line-height: 1.15 !important; margin-top: 0px !important;\">$\\mathbb{N}$</span>.</p></div></div></section></div><p><a href=\"https://gateoverflow.in/532602\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Official discussion page</a></p>"},{"n":9,"type":"MSQ","section":"Technical","tags":"","html":"<p>Let $\\mathbb{N}=\\{0,1,2,3, \\ldots\\}$ denote the set of natural numbers, and let $\\mathcal{P}(\\mathbb{N})$ denote its power set. Which of the following functions successfully serves as a valid witness to establish the cardinality relationship $|\\mathbb{N}| \\leq|\\mathcal{P}(\\mathbb{N})|$ ? (Select all that apply)</p>","paragraph":null,"options":[{"key":"A","html":"<p>$f_{A}: \\mathbb{N} \\rightarrow \\mathcal{P}(\\mathbb{N}) \\quad$ where $\\quad f_{A}(x)=x^{2}$<br>&nbsp;</p>"},{"key":"B","html":"<p>$f_{B}: \\mathbb{N} \\rightarrow \\mathcal{P}(\\mathbb{N})$ where $f_{B}(x)=\\left\\{x^{2}\\right\\}$<br>&nbsp;</p>"},{"key":"C","html":"<p>$f_{C}: \\mathbb{N} \\rightarrow \\mathcal{P}(\\mathbb{N}) \\quad$ where $\\quad f_{C}(x)=\\left\\{y \\in \\mathbb{N} \\mid y \\neq x^{2}\\right\\}$<br>&nbsp;</p>"},{"key":"D","html":"<p>$f_{D}: \\mathbb{N} \\rightarrow \\mathcal{P}(\\mathbb{N})$ where $f_{D}(x)=\\{x, x+1\\}$</p>"}],"answer":[],"natRange":null,"answerText":"","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0},"solution":"<p><strong>Answer:</strong> Not available</p><p><em>No solution was available on the source discussion page.</em></p><p><a href=\"https://gateoverflow.in/532601\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Official discussion page</a></p>","keyMissing":true},{"n":10,"type":"MSQ","section":"Technical","tags":"","html":"<p>Let $\\mathbb{Z}^{+}$denote the set of positive integers, and let $\\aleph_{0}$ represent its cardinality (countably infinite). For any arbitrary non-empty sets $A, B$, and $C$, let $|A|$ denote the cardinality of set $A$, and let $A-B$ denote the set difference.<br><br>Which of the following is/are true?</p>","paragraph":null,"options":[{"key":"A","html":"<p>If $A$ is an infinite set, then there exists a subset $S \\subseteq A$ such that $|S|=\\aleph_{0}$.<br>&nbsp;</p>"},{"key":"B","html":"<p>There is no infinite set $A$ such that $|A|&lt;\\aleph_{0}$.<br>&nbsp;</p>"},{"key":"C","html":"<p>The set cardinality relation $\\leq$ satisfies transitivity: if $|A| \\leq|B|$ and $|B| \\leq |C|$, then $|A| \\leq|C|$.<br>&nbsp;</p>"},{"key":"D","html":"<p>If $A$ is an uncountably infinite set and $B$ is a countable set, then the set difference $A-B$ must be uncountably infinite.</p>"}],"answer":[],"natRange":null,"answerText":"","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0},"solution":"<p><strong>Answer:</strong> Not available</p><p><em>No solution was available on the source discussion page.</em></p><p><a href=\"https://gateoverflow.in/532599\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Official discussion page</a></p>","keyMissing":true}],"sectionsMeta":[{"name":"Technical","marks":15.0,"instructions":null}],"math":true,"scoring":"marks","sections":[{"name":"Technical","start":0,"count":10}],"stats":{"questions":10,"maxMarks":3,"types":{"MSQ":6,"MCQ":4},"withoutKey":8}};
